rognostic impact of calcitonin doubling times in patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma.", "VANDETANIB:Vandetanib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor used in the treatment of advanced medullary thyroid carcinoma.", "CABOZANTINIB:Cabozantinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that has shown benefits in patients with progressive medullary thyroid carcinoma.", "CARCINOEMBRYONIC ANTIGEN:Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is a tumor marker utilized in the medical field, particularly in the assessment of various cancers, including medullary thyroid carcinoma. It is often used in conjunction with calcitonin to evaluate the prognosis of patients diagnosed with this specific type of cancer. CEA serves as an important indicator in monitoring disease progression and treatment response in affected individuals.", "63 PACIENTES:The study evaluated 63 patients who underwent surgery for medullary thyroid carcinoma, providing data on their outcomes.", "BRITISH JOURNAL OF CANCER:The British Journal of Cancer is a peer-reviewed medical journal dedicated to publishing research on various aspects of cancer. It encompasses a wide range of topics, including clinical studies, meta-analyses, treatment, prevention, and epidemiology. Notably, the journal also features studies focused on specific types of cancer, such as medullary thyroid carcinoma outcomes. Through its comprehensive coverage, the British Journal of Cancer serves as a significant resource for researchers and healthcare professionals in the field of oncology.", "VANDATENIB:A drug used in clinical trials for treating locally advanced or metastatic medullary thyroid cancer, as part of a randomized, double-blind phase III trial.", "ORTIGA-CARVALHO TM:A researcher who has studied thyroid hormone receptors and resistance to thyroid hormone disorders.", "PHASE III TRIAL:A clinical trial phase that tests the effectiveness of a treatment in a larger group of patients, such as the trial involving Vandetanib for medullary thyroid cancer.", "HYPERTIROXINEMIA:A condition characterized by elevated levels of thyroxine in the blood, which can occur during pregnancy or due to other thyroid disorders."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}